<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      The one colour the platform needs before Compose exists.

      `android:windowBackground` is what a window is filled with before the first
      frame is drawn, and what shows through while one is torn down and rebuilt —
      a cold start, and every activity recreation. `LocaleController` documents
      the second case from the other side: a language change used to produce two
      teardowns, and what flashed through the aurora both times was this colour.

      It was `#0B0620`, written into the application's theme, and that is not a
      Castivio colour at all — no palette entry has that value. So the flash was
      a fourth background: darker and more purple than anything the backdrop
      draws, chosen once by hand and never compared with the thing it appears in
      front of.

      It is now `Palette.Deep`, the first stop of the backdrop's own gradient, so
      the frame before the aurora is the colour the aurora starts from.

      Stated twice, unavoidably: a Compose `Color` is a Kotlin value and a theme
      attribute is a resource, and there is no way to share one between them. So
      `check-invariants.sh` compares the two and fails if they drift, which is
      the next best thing to having one.
    -->
    <color name="castivio_window_background">#FF141031</color>
</resources>
